  {
    "knowledge_id": "1db27080-0b57-51d1-b44a-0239b5bbf687",
    "collection_name": "project_723f8d32efdb4604a9a94c9812bbec8b",
    "source_locator": "e01a70e9-5610-5410-9708-b80259bfdefe#page=1",
    "content": "Yet  these  performance  improvements  raise  a  critical question:  do  they  reflect  genuine  learning  facilitated  by effective  scaffolding,  or  do  they  co-occur  with  superficial  dependency  enabled  by  cognitive  offloading?  (Yan et  al.,  2025).  Scaffolding,  in  a  Vygotskian  sense,  is  temporary,  adaptive  support  that  helps  a  learner  master  a task  just  beyond  their  independent  ability.  In  contrast, cognitive  offloading  occurs  when  a  learner  delegates essential mental work to an external tool, bypassing the productive struggle (Leinwand et al., 2014; Kapur, 2008) necessary  to  build  robust,  long-term  knowledge  schemas (Jose et al., 2025). This concern is empirically noted as well. Studies now warn that students who rely heavily on GenAI often struggle to articulate why autogenerated code works (Jose et al., 2025) and score lower on delayed conceptual tests (Bastani et al., 2024). Observational data further reveal reductions in independent debugging and problem  decomposition-the  very  practices  thought  to cultivate durable expertise (Shihab et al., 2025). This tension  between  visible  success  and  invisible  understanding has been labelled the performance-learning paradox (Yan et al., 2025).",
