  {
    "knowledge_id": "5ef651db-df6d-5906-b714-1672f8810a22",
    "collection_name": "project_723f8d32efdb4604a9a94c9812bbec8b",
    "source_locator": "e01a70e9-5610-5410-9708-b80259bfdefe#page=16",
    "content": "This study offers a process-level, theory-building account of AI-assisted  learning  in  an  authentic  undergraduate  programming  context.  Anchored  in  Cognitive  Load Theory  and  Self-Determination  Theory,  the  proposed tension model describes how learners navigate between Domain  Mastery (concepts, explanation, evaluation) and  Tool  Mastery  (procedural  efficiency  with  AI),  and how boundary conditions (time pressure, task complexity/familiarity,  and  scaffolding  design)  may  be  associated with movement between a Scaffolding Loop and an Offloading  Loop.  Rather  than  adjudicating  efficacy,  our contribution  helps  explain  how  widely  reported  performance and affect gains can co-occur with thinner opportunities for germane processing and authorship, and why some students exhibit attenuated metacognitive calibration  when  offloading  becomes  routine.  Practically,  the model introduces specific strategies designed to leverage AI's benefits while strengthening the user's critical evaluation  and  reflection.  These  methods  include  dedicated 'critique-the-AI' phases, planned fading of AI assistance through offline tasks, verification journals, and contrastive  prompting.  Conceptually,  this  approach  shifts  the conversation from a simple 'use or ban' debate to a more nuanced discussion of how and when the tool aligns with learning goals and motivational needs.",
